<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="eyebrow">Status, with nothing rounded up</div>
		<h1>Devices</h1>
		<p class="lede">One phone is used every day. The other has no working modem, which
		means it is not a phone. Both are published; only one is recommended.</p>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr>
				<th>Device</th><th>SoC / port</th><th>State</th>
				<th>Not supported</th><th>Known issues</th>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>POCO X3 NFC<br><span class="mono" style="color:var(--accent);font-size:11.5px">xiaomi-surya</span></td>
				<td class="mono">SM7150-AC<br><span style="color:var(--mut);font-size:11.5px">qcom-sm7150</span></td>
				<td><span class="tag ok">daily driver</span></td>
				<td>Fingerprint reader &middot; stereo playback</td>
				<td>Echo on calls &middot; shutdown freeze without a root cause &middot;
				microphone level unconfirmed</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>POCO X3 Pro<br><span class="mono" style="color:var(--accent);font-size:11.5px">xiaomi-vayu</span></td>
				<td class="mono">SM8150-AC<br><span style="color:var(--mut);font-size:11.5px">own port</span></td>
				<td><span class="tag no">experimental</span></td>
				<td>Modem &middot; IPA firmware not redistributable</td>
				<td>The modem dies 2&ndash;4&nbsp;s after the RF is switched on</td>
			</tr>
		</table>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section>
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>01</span><h2>POCO X3 NFC &mdash; xiaomi-surya</h2></div>
		<p>The supported device. Cameras, in-call voice, sensors, GPS, both speakers, the
		panel at 60 and 120&nbsp;Hz, a power-off that powers off and a charge screen when
		it is plugged in while off.</p>

		<h3>Not supported, and not a matter of time</h3>
		<div class="grid">
			<div>
				<div class="t">Fingerprint reader</div>
				<p>Four routes were tried and all four end in the same place. The sensor
				speaks over a SPI bus that the secure world owns: the driver is in the
				TrustZone, not in Linux, and the SPI controller is not handed over.
				Reading the stock driver, replaying the transfers, the pre-TZ window and
				the vendor blob all end there. It needs a TrustZone exploit, which is a
				different project.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Stereo playback</div>
				<p>Both speakers work, and they play the same thing. The I2S bus to the
				amplifiers carries one channel. Splitting it is kernel work on
				<code>TERT_MI2S_RX</code>, not configuration &mdash; nothing in user space
				can produce a second channel that is not being transmitted.</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<h3>Known issues</h3>
		<div class="scroll">
		<table>
			<tr><th>Issue</th><th>Where it stands</th></tr>
			<tr><td>Echo on calls</td><td>The other end hears themselves. The canceller is
			wired and tunable at runtime; finding the values needs patience and a second
			phone.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Shutdown freeze</td><td>Powering off works, and the fix goes around the
			problem rather than curing it: the teardown still freezes, and why is still not
			explained.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Microphone level</td><td>It records. Whether the gain is right has not
			been measured against anything.</td></tr>
			<tr><td>GPS engine lock</td><td>Fixed here, and it is not this phone's fault:
			postmarketOS ships the engine locked on every Qualcomm device. The fix belongs
			upstream.</td></tr>
		</table>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="panel">
	<div class="wrap">
		<div class="h"><span>02</span><h2>POCO X3 Pro &mdash; xiaomi-vayu</h2></div>
		<p>Published as experimental, and the honest summary is one line: <b>the modem
		does not work, so it is not a phone.</b> It boots, the screen and the touch work,
		and that is where the good news stops.</p>
		<div class="grid">
			<div>
				<div class="t">The modem dies 2&ndash;4&nbsp;s after the RF is on</div>
				<p>The fault is pinned to a single firmware assertion
				(<code>rflm_qlnk_ls_retry_cnt &lt; 2</code>): the modem asserts and takes
				itself down. Everything measured on the way there is written up, with the
				DIAG instruments used to measure it.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">IPA firmware is not shipped</div>
				<p>It cannot be redistributed. A helper extracts it from the vendor
				partition of the phone you already own.</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<div class="t">Why publish it at all</div>
				<p>So the research kernel and the patched <code>qcom-diag</code> are
				installable by anyone who wants to carry the investigation further,
				instead of living in a folder.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
		<div class="next">
			<a href="features.html"><b>Next</b><span>What you get</span></a>
			<a href="install.html"><b>Or</b><span>Installation</span></a>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
